        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I finally had time to investigate this. Turned out to be not *that* complicated.\r\n\r\nVim's session file itself causes this error when file with folds is edited outside session in a way that removes expected folds. At the moment I think it is a good idea to keep these error messages in the sake of being more explicit and reduce usage surprise when `MiniSessions.read()` behaves differently than `:source`.\r\n\r\nAnother issue here is that `MiniSession.read()` doesn't work properly when session file throws error (it stops sourcing script entirely, see #75), but there seems to be nothing I can do within this plugin (again, see #75).\r\n\r\nReproducible example:\r\n- Open Neovim: `nvim --clean -u NONE`.\r\n- `:set foldmethod=indent`.\r\n- `:edit folded-file` and add lines `a`, `\\ta`, `\\ta`, `a`, `\\ta`, `\\ta` and save it with `:w`.\r\n- Type `zM` to close all folds.\r\n- Open first fold: `:2`, `zo`.\r\n- `:mksession! tmp-session` \r\n- `:q`.  Now `tmp-session` file should contain line `2` followed by `normal! zo`. Which means it will try to go to line 2 and open fold there. If no such fold is found (like if lines in 'folded-file' are erased completely), it will throw `E490: No fold found`.\r\n- `nvim --clean -u NONE folded-file`, erase all lines with `dG`, save and quit with `:x`.\r\n- `nvim --clean -u NONE` and `:source tmp-session`. This will throw the error.\r\n\r\nClosing, as this is not an error from 'mini.sessions'.",
            "created_at": "2022-05-09T15:33:31Z",
